# Ox visual design contract

Contract revision: **5.0 — Visual Studio 2026 Enterprise baseline**, 2026-11-17.
Previous 4.1 consolidation and dated observations remain historical below.
Documentation: English. Product interface: Brazilian Portuguese (pt-BR).
This is the maintained visual reference for Ox, subordinate to
[the constitution](docs/constitution.md), functional contracts and the SDD.
Design intent, native implementation and acceptance are separate outcomes.

## Current visual authority — 2026-11-17

**Ox must have VISUAL STUDIO 2026 ENTERPRISE as its visual base.** This explicit
maintainer decision governs future visual work: desktop IDE composition, compact
context chrome, artifact-first central workspace, docked contextual tools, document
tabs, explorer/inspector hierarchy, restrained separators and coherent command/status
placement. Enterprise identifies the requested reference product; it does not imply
an Enterprise-only palette or capabilities. Use the verified sources and limits in
[Windows design analysis](docs/design/windows-apps-analysis.md).

Windows/Fluent guidance supplies supporting principles and interaction guidance.
Visual Studio 2026 Enterprise supplies the primary visual direction. Earlier R2–R13,
IntelliJ-inspired compositions and source kits retain provenance and applicable
functional details, but cannot override this baseline. The reference is the full
Visual Studio IDE, not Visual Studio Code or a generic Windows settings page.

Translate the baseline into Ox's agent-first workflow and Rust/Iced renderer.
Preserve OX signature artwork, IBM Plex, semantic status roles and documented legacy
color values unless a later authorized token change names its replacement and
validation. Do not copy Microsoft marks, proprietary assets or undocumented pixel
values. No WinUI/XAML migration, Windows platform expansion or new backend feature
is selected by this documentary decision.

| Area | Required Ox interpretation | Evidence for a future native change |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Shell and workspace | compact commands, stable context, dominant working canvas; contextual docked panes | paired captures with same state, logical size, scale and theme |
| Color and themes | semantic surfaces and interaction states; accent restrained; status redundant in text/icon | rendered contrast and state comparisons in Light/Dark/high contrast |
| Elevation/materials | hierarchy through surface roles and separators; opaque readable fallback | active/inactive, fallback and reduced effects observation |
| Geometry and density | aligned controls, deliberate grouping, resizable panes; preserve reading/target size | resize, long paths, localization, scale and keyboard resize |
| Typography/icons | Plex hierarchy and consistent semantic line icons; accessible names | glyph fallback, truncation recovery, labels and focus |
| Navigation/commands | distinguish destination, selection and focus; expose actual available actions | keyboard traversal, focus restoration, disabled/empty/error cases |
| Motion | purposeful optional feedback; no essential animated information | reduced motion and interruption behavior |
| Accessibility | contrast, keyboard, visible focus, target reachability and native semantics | separate actual assistive-technology/platform observations |

This revision defines the target contract. It does not assert that current native
screens already match Visual Studio 2026 Enterprise or renew earlier acceptance.
Surface adoption and implementation boundaries are in [surfaces](docs/design/surfaces.md);
source disposition is in [reference coverage](docs/design/reference-coverage.md).

### Welcome identity successor — 2026-11-16

The supplied definitive OX identity is adopted for S01: Catalisador v2 symbol,
Valência lettering/composition and the cotton mascot with its approved Ferris tag.
The PNG signature and mascot are embedded, unchanged, under
`application/crates/ox-ui/assets/identity/`; that directory records provenance.
The signature is artwork, not a replacement for IBM Plex UI typography.

The enterprise desktop successor below supersedes this historical column layout.
Original observations remain in [identity report](docs/validation/welcome-identity-20261116/report.md).

### Enterprise desktop composition successor — 2026-11-16

S01 uses the entire native window: institutional context chrome, docked project
actions/real recent paths and a neutral primary canvas. Identity belongs to compact
chrome; the mascot is a subordinate empty-state illustration. The earlier floating
1080-unit card and 240-unit identity column are retired by explicit user request.
Below 900 logical units project navigation occupies available width; scrolling
retains actions/paths without reducing typography. S02 is a docked form.

Workbench context commands share one row at 1100+ logical width and use two
rows below it; a compact single-row status footer preserves working height.
Navigation uses group line icons and a selected side marker distinct from focus.
Workbench navigation, section headings and preferences use the same flat hierarchy:
restrained chrome, neutral surfaces, thin separators, readable compact controls
and contextual information. Existing IBM Plex fonts, controlled color values,
semantic status and theme roles remain authoritative. Definitive identity artwork
retains its original bytes, proportions and transparency. No IntelliJ capability
is inferred from the supplied reference screenshots. Current observation/limits:
[enterprise report](docs/validation/enterprise-visual-20261116/report.md).

## 1. Sources, authority and decision status

The supplied English v3.0 provides detailed legacy colors and typography proposals.
The supplied Portuguese v4.0.0 provides broader state semantics, surface coverage,
component contracts, QA and native handoff. This English consolidation incorporates
both; v4 was selected for coverage, not its version number. Source identities and
the reconciliation are recorded in
[the delivery review](docs/validation/visual-workbench-20261111/review.md).
Original archives remain unchanged in the maintainer's local reference directory.
They are neither build dependencies nor project authorities.

Historical design precedence is per surface: R2 supplies workbench/architecture/
agents/observability; R3 review/failure/focus; R4 entry/projects/tasks/settings;
R5 administration/appearance. R11/R12 amend the components actually changed.
R12 does not supersede every earlier surface. Historical exports are historical.

Design classifications:

| Classification | Meaning | Does not establish |
| --- | --- | --- |
| LOCKED | Preserve a documented legacy role/value unless explicitly revised | Current native validation |
| PROPOSED | Candidate direction requiring evaluation | Default adoption or approval |
| IMPLEMENTED | Present in a concrete identified consumer | Verification or acceptance |
| VALIDATED | A bounded case was tested with inputs, method and result | Other environments or overall acceptance |

These labels do not replace the five acceptance states in
[SDD evidence](sdd/evidence.md). A technical criterion approval is not a user's
acceptance decision. Prototype text cannot grant permissions or change scope.

## 2. Identity and experience

**Ox Signature Blue · Chromatic Engineering Workbench.** Ox is an agent-first,
local-first desktop engineering tool. Tasks organize intent, constraints, plans,
execution, artifacts, verification and decisions. The editor and artifact inspection
remain first-class work areas; task-first does not mean a ticket dashboard or a
chat-only application.

The recognizable composition combines slate-blue context chrome, restrained
navigation, neutral reading surfaces, compact tools and inspectable evidence.
Keep shell variation low, technical content flexible and motion minimal. Use a
capture → inspect → correct → compare loop, not a marketing-site aesthetic.

| Principle | Observable requirement |
| --- | --- |
| Information before decoration | File, task, status, source and consequence precede ornament |
| Color communicates a role | Brand, category, state, syntax and data remain separate |
| Professional density | Readable trees, tabs, tables and inspectors; no tiny-text workaround |
| Evidence remains inspectable | Unknown, inferred, verified and accepted are distinguishable |
| One system across views | Shared components with purposeful variants, not page-specific copies |
| Multiple signals | Critical states use text plus icon/shape, not color alone |
| Critical defects first | Fix clipping, inaccessible actions and false status before polish |

Avoid stock photography, decorative heroes/cards, glow, parallax, moving hover
targets and gradients across operational surfaces. New visualizations must serve
the artifact or task rather than compete with it.

## 3. Domain language and truth states

| Entity | Required context | Never infer |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Project | Directory, source and availability | An active execution |
| Task / plan / step | Objective, constraints, dependencies, status | Elapsed progress from step counts |
| Execution / attempt | Identity, origin, lifecycle and observed outcome | Successful work from successful process exit |
| Agent definition | Role, configuration and capabilities | A running instance |
| Agent activity | Definition, execution identity and current activity | Unrestricted authority |
| Change / artifact | Path/revision, contents and provenance | Verification or acceptance |
| Verification / evidence | Criterion, method, inputs, result and freshness | A human acceptance decision |
| Decision / permission | Request, scope, consequence and confirmation | Applied operation from a submitted request |
| Provider / model / context | Source, scope, unit and availability | Connectivity, real prices or unlimited context |

Required distinctions: implemented ≠ verified ≠ accepted; unknown ≠ inferred;
running ≠ successful; role ≠ state; requested ≠ confirmed; measured ≠ estimated;
zero ≠ unavailable; demonstration ≠ integrated backend.

| State | pt-BR presentation | Visual responsibility |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Planned / ready | Planejada / Pronta | Neutral; blue only for an available action |
| Running / verifying | Em execução / Verificando | Operational teal plus explicit activity |
| Waiting | Aguardando | Neutral, with reason when known |
| Blocked / permission required | Bloqueada / Autorização necessária | Amber when intervention is required |
| Failed / verification failed | Falhou / Verificação falhou | Red, diagnosis and recovery consequence |
| Cancellation requested | Cancelamento solicitado | Pending; wait for observed termination |
| Cancelled | Cancelada | Neutral; no automatic rollback claim |
| Implemented | Implementada | Neutral; never verification green |
| Verified | Verificada | Green plus criterion/test/evidence |
| Accepted | Aceita | Explicit decision label and recorded decision |
| Inferred | Inferida | Violet, dashed relation and origin/hypothesis |
| Unknown | Desconhecida / Não confirmado | Neutral; never inference violet |
| Estimated | Estimada | Label, method, unit and source |

Expose the applicable chain: source → task → execution/attempt → change →
verification → evidence → decision. Gaps remain gaps: show “Sem evidência
disponível”, “Não integrado” or “Não confirmado”, not invented IDs or links.
Technical identifiers and raw diagnostics can retain their original language;
surround them with localized explanation and preserve the original bytes.

## 4. Color roles and preserved Light palette

The following legacy values and meanings are preserved exactly. Preserving a
value does not require using every legacy background as the default canvas.

| Role | Value | Use |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Shell | #243C59 | Institutional chrome |
| Deep shell | #182535 | Recessed rail/context |
| Action / strong | #28679F / #205483 | Primary actions and interaction |
| Muted blue | #6E9FCF | Secondary graphics, not small text on white |
| Highlight / soft blue | #B1D0F0 / #D5E7F9 | Restricted contextual highlights |
| Selection | #E8F2FD | Selected item plus a solid marker |
| Legacy canvas / tool | #EDF3FA / #F6F9FD | Compatibility, not mandatory global tint |
| Editor / base | #FFFFFF | Reading and artifact inspection |
| Primary / secondary / muted text | #263548 / #43566E / #52657C | Content hierarchy |
| Shell text | #F6FAFE | Text on branded chrome |
| Decorative / soft border | #CBD8E6 / #E4ECF5 | Nonessential separators |
| Success / background | #226B50 / #EDF6F0 | Confirmed verification; diff addition |
| Warning / background | #80521D / #FFF4E3 | Relevant risk or intervention |
| Error / background | #A53848 / #FCEEF1 | Failure, destructive effect, diff deletion |
| Inference / background | #775C8B / #F3EEF7 | Explicit hypothesis |

Native Light candidate A uses neutral navigation #F2F4F6, workbench #F7F8FA,
recessed headers #E8ECEF and editor white. Essential input boundaries use
#7A8798; decorative borders do not substitute for functional boundaries.
Success/warning/error midtones #2F8060/#A26720/#C14B58 and borders
#8BBAA4/#D9B579/#DFA4AC are candidate emphasis, not replacements for legacy text.

Domain colors are execution #216C73, architecture #5D5E9F and impact #985F40,
with soft backgrounds #EAF5F3/#EEEFFA/#F8F1EA. They identify context, not
success, inference or warning. Use small markers rather than full-panel tints.

Chromatic direction B (code teal #2B7078/#E7F2F1 and document mineral
#6E6259/#F6F3EF) remains PROPOSED, as do its Dark/HC counterparts. It must not
replace brand/action/focus or be enabled by an inherited prototype flag.
Direction C adds categories only for an evidenced discrimination need.
Compare the same fixture before adoption; percentage of colored pixels is not
a quality metric. Do not fabricate a second chart series to justify a color.

## 5. Token and theme architecture

Decision flow: DESIGN.md → [controlled tokens](docs/design/tokens.json) →
generated native sRGB roles → component variants → observed QA.
The source JSON preserves all supplied legacy tokens and all three 63-role
theme maps. Compatibility aliases remain until consumers are inspected.

Primitives define colors/spacing/radii/type; semantic roles define meaning;
components map roles to states; themes override values without changing roles;
density changes geometry without altering meaning. Runtime Iced does not parse
CSS, color-mix or OKLCH. Generated Rust must agree with the source JSON.

Light is the initial implementation candidate. Dark and High Contrast are explicit
session-selectable native candidates, not automatically inverted Light or claims
of OS integration. Test hover, pressed, disabled, selection, focus, overlays, diff,
graphs and status independently. Prefer OS accessibility colors only when actually
available; otherwise use the explicit HC palette and report that limitation.
Syntax/editor appearance is a separate future preference; it must not accidentally
change evidence semantics. Settings persist only when storage is implemented.

## 6. Typography, microcopy and assets

IBM Plex Sans/Mono remains the visual baseline. The native application embeds
licensed native font files when available; fallback must be explicit. Inter and
JetBrains Mono remain an A/B proposal until an adopted decision and comparison
of real content, metrics, DPI and fallback demonstrate suitability.

| Role | Logical size baseline | Behavior |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Task/workspace heading | 22–24 | Semibold, no marketing hero |
| Page/dialog heading | 20–22 | Clear hierarchy |
| Panel / body | 15–16 | Readable explanations and state |
| Button / tree / table | 14–15 | Stable targets and localized labels |
| Metadata | 13 | 12 only after contextual review |
| Code / diff | 15, approximately 26 line height | Monospaced; no silent shrinking |
| Trace | 13–14 | Monospaced where useful |
| Metric | 18–28 | Unit, period, provenance and tabular numbers |
| Graph label | 12–14 | Inspect/expand full identifier |

Compare 0/O, 1/l/I, braces, operators, accented pt-BR, numeric columns, long
paths and missing glyphs. Code ligatures are disabled unless deliberately adopted.
Microcopy states action, consequence and certainty. Prefer “Ver origem” or
“Inspecionar falha” over generic “Sucesso” or “Saiba mais”. Unknown values are
“Indisponível”, not zero; times/costs need unit, period, timezone and source.

Use original role-based line icons with consistent optical weight, typically
16–20 logical px, and text/tooltip on every otherwise ambiguous command. Test
small/large rasterization and fallback. Reuse licensed supplied assets only after
checking compatibility; preserve notices and source identities. Neither a supplied
SVG nor a font filename proves permission, glyph coverage or native rendering.

## 7. Geometry, density and adaptive behavior

Use a 4-unit spacing baseline (4/8/12/16/20/24/32/40), adjusted for actual content.
Start with 48–52 context chrome, 36–44 tool headers, approximately 32 controls,
28–32 rows, 240–320 navigation and 280–360 inspector. These are starting
constraints, not hardcoded universal dimensions. Radii: 0 for docked editor,
4 for chips, 6 for controls, 8 for menus; avoid large pill-shaped containers.
Workbench depth is flat plus thin separators; shadows belong to actual overlays.

Comfortable is default. Compact reduces gaps/padding/rows, preserving text,
code size, essential labels and focus. Session-only selection is explicit.

| Logical window | Layout strategy |
| --- | --- |
| 640×480 and ≤1024 wide | Collapse secondary panels; keep navigation reachable and critical actions scrollable |
| 1366×768 | Compact tool arrangement; avoid simultaneous competing sidebars |
| 1440×900 | Review baseline; primary work area with contextual tools |
| 1920×1080 | Multipane layout with controlled reading width |
| 2560×1080 / 2560×1440 | Distribute tools without inflating controls or extremely long text |

Test 1×/1.25×/1.5×/2× when applicable. These are desktop windows, not mobile
breakpoints. Mouse and touch target requirements are different. A collapsed panel
must preserve content and have a discoverable keyboard-accessible restoration path.
Restore persisted geometry only when persistence really exists, validating screen bounds.

## 8. Component contracts

Each family specifies purpose, consumers, variants, semantic roles, domain and
interaction states, input/event contract, constraints, keyboard, content limits,
theme/density behavior, fixtures and tests. States include default, hover,
pressed, focus, selected and disabled; data states include empty/loading/error/
unknown/stale when meaningful. Do not mechanically add irrelevant states.

| Family | Required behavior |
| --- | --- |
| Context chrome / status bar | Project/task/environment and actual availability; no invented branch or usage |
| Navigation / explorer | Selected marker distinct from keyboard focus; visible labels, overflow and collapse |
| Tabs | Active marker, dirty indicator and diagnostics remain different; keyboard-accessible overflow |
| Primary button | Blue action role, tested text contrast, visible hover/pressed/focus |
| Secondary / toolbar | Neutral or contextual shell; meaningful labels; no layout shift |
| Destructive action | Explicit verb and consequence; confirmation follows actual operation contract |
| Input / selector | Persistent label, value/help/error; visible boundary; placeholder is not the label |
| Dialog / command palette | Purpose, options, consequences, initial focus, traversal, Escape and focus return |
| Settings | Stable categories and property/help/control layout; preview never implies persistence |
| Task / plan | Intent, identity, constraints, steps/dependencies and discrete actual status |
| Agent | Definition and instance separated, with execution identity and availability |
| Trace | Event origin, order, certainty and loss status; raw output remains recoverable when captured |
| Editor / diff | Path/revision/line/hunk; additions/deletions also have symbols; no hidden critical identifier |
| Problems / tests | Source, criterion/case, severity/result and partial/unknown cases |
| Architecture / impact | Node/relation types, provenance, inferred dashed edges and inspectable alternative table |
| Observability | Measured/derived/estimated/unavailable, source/unit/period; unknown is not zero |
| Decision / evidence | Scope, consequence, request/confirmation, criterion, freshness and recorded observation |
| Failure / recovery | Known failure versus uncertainty; safe actions only when supported; no invented rollback |

Graph controls require their own hit testing, keyboard and pan/zoom contracts when
implemented. Do not claim large-graph performance, streaming or virtualization from
a mockup. Synthetic examples must be visibly labeled and never replace backend data.

## 9. Surface coverage and native integration

The complete implementation/source map is maintained in
[surface contracts](docs/design/surfaces.md). Each destination links to project
requirements and records backend availability separately from presentation.

| Design family | Focus and required distinction |
| --- | --- |
| S01 Start | Workspace selection, recent/resume availability; no fictitious recent history |
| S02 New project | Source/path/consequence; no simulated clone/create confirmation |
| S03 Project | Context and tasks; actual filesystem validation distinguished from indexing |
| S04/S05 Tasks/create | Goal, constraints, plan, execution prerequisites and actual supported task |
| S06 Activity | Ordered attempt/verification history and observation freshness |
| S07 Decisions | Pending requests, scope and confirmed response; no implied consent |
| S08/S09 Agents/config | Definitions versus running instances; unavailable providers explicit |
| S10 Providers/models | Availability, data transmission scope and source; no invented prices |
| S11 Context | Query scope, snapshot/provenance/freshness; no invented index hits |
| S12 Tools/plugins | Capability/permission/host contract; an API is not desktop integration |
| S13/S14 Settings/appearance | Actual session preferences and separately planned durable settings |
| W00 Workbench/editor | Primary artifact canvas plus contextual supervision and evidence |
| A01 Architecture | Structural/exploratory modes of the same provenance-bound logical graph |
| A02 Multi-agent | Roles, dependencies, execution and verified results kept separate |
| A03 Observability | Source-bound metrics; explicit empty/unavailable state |
| A04 Review | Changes, tests, evidence and separate acceptance decision |
| A05 Recovery | Failure diagnosis, uncertain effects and preservation boundaries |
| A06 Focus | Task/artifact concentration with reversible panel collapse |
| TW tools | Problems/tests/trace/terminal/files presentation with availability per service |
| SDD / skills | Portable files as authority; indication/selection/consultation/use distinguished |

Current local task execution remains functional. Future engines receive native
presentation and explicit integration contracts; navigation alone never starts
a process or remote operation. Unimplemented effects stay disabled with an explanation.

## 10. Accessibility and localization

Use [WCAG 2.2](https://www.w3.org/TR/2024/REC-WCAG22-20241212/) as a precisely
scoped reference for contrast, color, keyboard, focus, labels and errors, adapted
to the native platform. Normal text target is 4.5:1, qualifying large text 3:1,
essential nontext boundaries 3:1. Decorative separators are not functional borders.
Computed opaque pairs do not establish effective alpha/rendering or conformance.

Keep logical Tab/Shift+Tab order, Enter/Space activation, visible unobscured focus
and discoverable scrolling. Escape closes the active overlay and returns focus;
it never silently cancels a task or approves an operation. Remove hidden controls
from traversal and preserve valid focus after navigation/resize/busy changes.
Critical commands remain reachable without a mouse. Tooltips are supplementary.

Use text plus shape/icon for semantic state. Error messages name the operation
and next supported action. Do not truncate the sole source/decision identifier;
provide wrapping, expansion or horizontal scrolling where appropriate.
No essential animation; future motion honors reduced-motion preferences.
Screen-reader/native-accessibility support requires actual platform observation;
custom focus and screenshots do not establish it. Linux X11/Wayland remain separate
targets; inherited Windows-oriented recommendations do not expand platform scope.

## 11. Visual resilience and performance

Exercise long localized strings, deep paths, Unicode, many retained observations,
font fallback, missing data, held verification, cancellation, late replies and
small windows. Preserve raw diagnostics and data; never solve overflow by dropping
evidence. Nonblocking view construction and viewport-aware composition remain required.
Animation defaults to none; any transition must have a purpose and must not delay action.
No performance advantage is claimed from native Rust, fewer colors or planned
virtualization. Existing F0 budgets are not revalidated by these visual changes.

## 12. Review and acceptance protocol

1. Identify actual source/revision, fixture, state, dependencies and current native baseline.
2. Classify findings: P0 certainty/access failure, P1 blocked operation, P2 friction,
   P3 polish. Fix critical semantics and focus before chromatic refinement.
3. State a bounded hypothesis and map roles/components/states. Retain proposal status.
4. Compare identical data, size, scale, theme and density. Record raw captures and inputs.
5. Run relevant static checks, actual rendering, keyboard and functional journeys.
6. Inspect captures at native logical scale, correct causes and recapture affected cases.
7. Record result and limitations per criterion; absence of a check never becomes PASS.

The source kit proposes seven families × three themes × two densities (42 cases).
This is a planning reference, not evidence or an imported quota. The native protocol
selects cases from actual surfaces, changed states and risk, reports the denominator
and distinguishes captured, inspected, interacted and assistive-tested coverage.
Similarly, source-kit 95% tokenization is a proposal: measure an explicit eligible
denominator before using a percentage; no unreviewed exception is silently accepted.

Stop claiming completion where critical defects or missing evidence remain.
Design review, native implementation and product acceptance need their own records.
Use issue/feature/task records already available; a PR or external reviewer is not
a prerequisite for authorized local work. Human visual preference and assistive
technology checks remain explicit when not observed.

## 13. Handoff, maintenance and responsibilities

For each component record: ID, purpose, source, variants, roles, domain/interaction
states, geometry/type constraints, keyboard, accessibility, data source, fixture,
native implementation, screenshot/test and unresolved decision. Maintain reverse
traceability token → widget → surface → evidence.

Design decisions own roles and appearance; domain contracts own facts and effects;
native engineering owns renderer integration; QA records observations. Do not invent
named reviewer approvals. The contributor updates affected docs before task closure,
following [the workflow](sdd/workflow.md).

Use the installed skill catalog selectively. Native-specific guidance lives in
iced-engineering; general component and usability methods remain design-system and
design-review. No external archive script, generated CSS or `.agents/skills` tree
is an automatic operational dependency.

## 14. Blocking review failures

Do not approve loss of locked identity, homogeneous blue tint, false success,
inference-colored unknown, color-only status, unreadable essential text, clipped
critical action, inaccessible focus, invented backend data, broken token generation,
misattributed screenshots, untested conformance/performance claims or unlicensed assets.

## 15. External technical references and limits

[Iced 0.14.0](https://docs.rs/iced/0.14.0/iced/) and its installed sources establish
the native API contract. [IBM Plex](https://github.com/IBM/plex) supplies font
provenance and its OFL notice; actual bundled identities are recorded with assets.
WCAG applicability and measurement limits are specified above. Competitor examples
in the source kit are inspiration, not proprietary palettes/assets to copy or
proof of suitability. Taste/Impeccable-inspired inspection methods were evaluated
through the supplied original Ox entries; no upstream license or implementation
effectiveness is inferred from their names.

## 16. Delivery status

Native implementation and verification are recorded in
[product-visual-workbench](sdd/features/product-visual-workbench/converge.md).
Reusable method integration has separate acceptance in
[skill-native-visual-method](sdd/features/skill-native-visual-method/spec.md).
The existence of this contract does not approve the product, all themes, future
engines or assistive-technology support.

Native shared contracts: [components](docs/design/components.md). Font identity and distribution notice: [provenance](docs/design/font-provenance.json).

## 17. Reference integration successor

The [source trace](docs/design/reference-coverage.md) maps v3/v4 sections and source
clauses to this contract, with package disposition and explicit exclusions.
The embedded document workbench now includes file selection, matching tabs, line
numbers, a primary scrollable code canvas, origin inspector and contextual tools.
It is a labelled read-only demonstration; selecting a tab performs no project IO.
Wide layout reserves auxiliary columns only when the primary canvas remains usable;
narrow layout stacks sections. Focus mode retains access without a three-column lock.
Architecture presents declared Cargo dependencies with directional shape and source;
it is not a live graph query. Agent role tables separate responsibility from instances.

Requirements retained for later integration: tab dirty state differs from active/error;
conversation identifies human/agent/system and send failure; plans show discrete steps
without fabricated progress; trace rows carry attempt/time/source/loss; diff carries
revision/hunk/line; Git status uses letters/symbols independently of verification.
These details remain planned, not silently discarded or declared implemented.
Dedicated graph pan/zoom, split-pane resizing, editing and persistent settings remain
separate integration work. Historical prototype quotas and approval rituals are not
imported as policy. All existing legacy and R13 proposal classifications remain.

Preferences successor: S13/S14 present session scope, theme and density as the
primary content. Typography/color/state specimens are disclosed by the existing
Details control. Task-evidence badges and backend-engine notices do not appear
in the normal preferences flow.

### Desktop navigation successor — 2026-11-17

The UI no longer renders the mascot; original artwork remains preserved. Settings
at the upper right is an icon-only32-unit target with real hover tooltip and
visible keyboard-focus border. Its label appears only on hover as requested;
other chrome/rail icons retain focus labels. Shared top chrome is48 units with16-unit line icons, compact
signature/context and commands; content still uses IBM Plex at established sizes.

Each of27 destinations has a distinct native semantic icon. Grouped navigation
appears once per category with stable IDs and aligned keyboard order. Expanded
sidebar defaults256, drags between200 and420, further limited by window width
minus326 to reserve a320-unit primary area and6-unit divider. Collapsed rail52
retains destinations/tooltips/focus; expansion restores the session's preferred
width. Window resize clamps the visible width. No geometry is persisted.

Drag ends on release, cursor leaving the window, focus loss, Escape, navigation
or resize. Ctrl+B toggles; sidebar shrink/grow buttons support Tab/Enter24-unit
steps. Ctrl+Alt+Left/Right is supplementary and may be consumed by the window
manager. Actual geometry/pointer/keyboard evidence is in the navigation report.

### Project-entry correction — 2026-11-17

Settings controls always use the same cog, including agent settings and destination
navigation. S01 actions follow Novo projeto → Abrir pastas… → Clonar repositório.
Clone remains disabled with an unavailable tooltip until implemented. No manual-path
menu action is shown; chooser failure can disclose its recovery field. Recent
projects and current-session resumption belong in the primary area. Below 900
logical units, actions precede that area in one scrollable vertical composition.

### Fixed sidebar/settings successor — 2026-11-17

S01 keeps its actions column docked for the full content height with Plugins in
a separate bottom area; only its upper actions and primary project list scroll.
At narrow widths the sidebar is 210 units, retaining the bottom destination.
S13/S14 use a dedicated settings canvas without the general navigation/chrome.
Voltar and Salvar remain outside scrolling content at bottom right. Theme/density
preview immediately; Save retains session values and returns, Back/Escape cancel.
No preference persistence or plugin runtime integration is introduced.
